Subject: Re: AcmePay onboarding: SDK setup, traffic smoothing & rate handling

Hi Sarah —

Thanks — short answers below and a few practical examples you can use in the workshop. Attaching rate_limit_matrix.csv with suggested starting values based on your burst description.

1) Guardrail approach (recommendation):
- Token-bucket per-tenant with a burst allowance = baseline_rate * 8 for ~3 minute windows.
- Sustained ceiling = baseline_rate * 2.
- Short-circuit on queue depth: if queue > 200 requests for >30s trigger immediate canary rollback.

2) Client-side retry guidance (idempotent-ish endpoints):
- Exponential backoff with jitter; max retries = 3
- Timeout = 8s per request for blocking calls; streaming calls keep keepalive 60s
- On 429: retry-after header respected; implement linear backoff starting at 250ms + jitter

3) Endpoint migration / zero-downtime pattern:
- Step A: Deploy new endpoint as /v2/endpoint on Redwood side behind a feature flag.
- Step B: Point 5% traffic (canary) via routing rule — observe error budget and latency over 30m windows.
- Step C: Gradually increase to 25% -> 50% -> 100% with monitoring gates (error rate, 95p latency, cost variance).
- Rollback: switch routing back to /v1 and pause autoscale triggers; typical rollback < 5 minutes.

Sample Node snippet (use SDK client v0.9+):

const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference')
const client = new Redwood({apiKey: process.env.RWD_KEY, timeout: 8000})

async function callModel(input) {
  try {
    return await client.generate({model: 'rw-medium', prompt: input, maxTokens: 512})
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.status === 429 && err.headers['retry-after']) {
      // respect server-side backoff
      const wait = Number(err.headers['retry-after']) * 1000
      await sleep(wait + jitter(200))
      // one retry after wait
      return client.generate({model: 'rw-medium', prompt: input, maxTokens: 512})
    }
    throw err
  }
}

Operational knobs we suggest enabling during cutover:
- KV prefix caching for repeated prompts (saves CPU and tokens)
- Adaptive batching (start at batchSize: 4; monitor latency and increase if stable)
- Traffic smoothing layer in front of the SDK to shape short bursts into a steady flow for 30s windows

Links: https://redwood.internal/docs/sdk-setup (SDK wiring)

Attachments: rate_limit_matrix.csv, sdk_sample.patch (same patch Vanessa shared)

Happy to walk through these in the workshop and calibrate numbers to your telemetry.

